\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Chosen perfect symmetric pairings}
\date{2026-10-20}

\p{The \vocabk{perfect counting duality}{fdip-001F} represents every
linear functional by a unique field. To make the same construction on
another carrier, let #{R} be a commutative unital \vocabk{ring}{ca-000U}
and #{M} a unital \vocabk{#{R}-module}{ca-000X}. Write
#{M^\vee=\operatorname{Hom}_R(M,R)} for its
\vocabk{dual module}{ca-0012}. Every linear map here has coefficient
map #{\operatorname{id}_R}, as in \ref{ca-001G}.}

\p{A \newvocab{chosen perfect symmetric pairing} on #{M} is a
\vocabk{bilinear form}{ca-0003} #{b_M:M\times M\to R} such that
#{b_M(x,y)=b_M(y,x)} and the linear map
##{\Phi_M:M\longrightarrow M^\vee,\qquad
   \Phi_M(y)(x)=b_M(x,y)}
is an \vocabk{isomorphism}{ca-0015}. Thus each functional #{\ell}
has exactly one representative #{\Phi_M^{-1}\ell}, characterized by
#{b_M(x,\Phi_M^{-1}\ell)=\ell(x)} for every #{x}. Bilinearity makes
#{\Phi_M} linear. Symmetry identifies the other currying map
#{y\mapsto (x\mapsto b_M(y,x))} with #{\Phi_M}, so both are
isomorphisms. This agrees with the two-sided perfect-pairing convention
in Mathlib's \leanref{LinearMap.IsPerfPair}. The ordinary Hom convention
is \citet{Section 10.10, Tag 0581}{stacks2017stacks}.}

\p{Injectivity alone says that #{b_M(x,y)=0} for every #{x} forces
#{y=0}; symmetry gives the analogous assertion in the other slot.
It does not give every functional a representative; see
[a zero-radical pairing that is not perfect](fdip-001S).}

\p{No finiteness, basis or projectivity hypothesis is imposed on #{M};
the specified duality is the assumption. The counting form on every
finite function module satisfies it by the explicit inverse in
\ref{fdip-001F}. The zero module has its unique perfect pairing.
If #{R} is the zero ring, every unital module is zero since
#{x=1x=0x=0}, so this case is included. Perfection does not assert
positivity or determine a bilinear form from its diagonal values.}
